Qwaszx86 wrote:
>
> Ok, I know lots of people have different definitions of what
> constitutes "early game" and "mid game" but basically, I am always
> dying around the same place in the game (or earlier). *Usually* I
> complete either the mines or Sokoban, sometimes both. By the end I am
> *usually* level 10 or so. I die for all sorts of reasons, but usually
> around this part of the game.

The type of skills you need to acquire change as the game
progresses.  Since you often survive the early game, you've
acquired the types of skills needed in the early game.  Now
it's time to start all over again learning new types of skills.
This is one of the reasons Nethack holds my interest across
the years - there are always new skills to learn.

> I guess I have several questions. Around
> the time I hit level 10, I start feeling underpowered, my AC isn't low
> enough (usually -5 at best),

That's not too bad.  Just to check - You clean out every level,
drag every piece of armor to the minetown altar to check b/u/c
status, and try on every single piece of b/u armor except
ones that might auto-curse (helmets).

> I don't have any artifacts because I haven't found a co-aligned alter,

There's always the minetown altar.  It's co-aligned 1/3rd of
the time.  And if you haven't found an unattended altar down
to Medusa it's likely worth killing the priest and handling the
price.

Finding altars matters, but it is indeed random.  Just to check -
You normally play neutrals?  Every lawfull has an excellent
chance at Excalibur.  Every chaotic can have either of two
artifacts and those aren't even limited to chaotics, not that they
are really usefull artifacts.

> I don't have enough stuff ID'd that
> could save me. Any suggestions on how I could make the transition?

Learning better ID skills seems the next step.  Many like
price IDing but I think it's over rated after a while.

For potions I tend to go into a room in mine town, close
the door, and quaff them.  There aren't that many types with
bad effects and once you've learned them and you've survived
the list gets smaller.  Also monsters will throw the bad ones
at you so you learn that way.  I'm a bit more conservative
about reading scrolls but I often end up reading them in a
closed room as well, but I like to know some of the worst
ones first.

Wands may be the most important to ID.  Learn about
enrave identification.

